Response: content='The jurisdictional boundaries of North American power utilities are defined by a
combination of **federal, state, and local regulatory frameworks**, with authority distributed
across multiple agencies. Key points from the retrieved documents include:\n\n---\n\n### ✅ **Primary
Jurisdictional Authorities**\n\n1. **Federal Energy Regulatory Commission (FERC)**  \n   - Oversees
**interstate electricity transmission**, wholesale power markets, and **natural gas pipelines**.\n
- Regulates **electric utilities engaged in interstate commerce**.\n   - Has authority over
**transmission rates, grid reliability, and market rules**.\n   - **Exemptions**: Utilities using
**non-fossil-fuel cogeneration** or **non-utility generation** may be exempt from certain FERC
regulations under the **Public Utility Regulatory Policies Act (PURPA)**.\n\n2. **State Public
Service Commissions (PSCs) or Utility Regulatory Commissions**  \n   - Govern **local utilities**
and **intrastate operations**.\n   - Set **retail electricity rates**, approve infrastructure
projects, and enforce service standards.\n   - Conduct **public hearings** and balance the needs of
utilities, consumers, and government bodies.\n   - Promote **energy conservation** through time-of-
use rates, demand response programs, and incentives.\n\n3. **Federal Communications Commission
(FCC)**  \n   - While not a power utility regulator, the FCC has jurisdiction over
**telecommunications infrastructure** that may be co-located with power systems (e.g., fiber optics
on utility poles).\n   - Regulates **rights-of-way**, **interconnection**, and **access to utility
infrastructure**.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ **Utility Jurisdictional Boundaries**\n\n- **Electric utilities**
are typically defined as entities that:\n  - Install, operate, and maintain **electric supply
systems** (generation, transmission, distribution).\n  - Are **recognized by public service
commissions or utility commissions**.\n  - May include **co-generation facilities** or **non-fossil-
fuel energy providers** under PURPA exemptions.\n\n- **Jurisdictional control** applies to:\n  -
**Service drops and laterals** (wires from the utility to the customer).\n  - **Metering
equipment**.\n  - **Easements, rights-of-way**, or **agreements** recognized by regulatory
agencies.\n  - **Property owned or leased by the utility** for energy-related purposes (e.g.,
generation, transmission, distribution).\n\n- **Exclusions**: Utilities do **not** have jurisdiction
over:\n  - Equipment **not under their exclusive control** (e.g., customer-owned wiring).\n  -
**Non-utility installations** unless they are part of a regulated interconnection.\n\n---\n\n### ✅
**Key Regulatory Frameworks**\n\n| Agency | Jurisdiction | Key Responsibilities
|\n|-------|--------------|------------------------|\n| **FERC** | Interstate power transmission |
Wholesale rates, grid reliability, market oversight |\n| **State PSCs** | Intrastate utilities |
Retail rates, service standards, infrastructure approval |\n| **FCC** | Telecommunications
infrastructure | Rights-of-way, pole attachments, broadband access |\n| **PURPA** | Cogeneration &
renewable energy | Mandates utility purchase of power from qualifying facilities |\n\n---\n\n### ✅
**Summary**\n\nNorth American power utilities operate under **multi-tiered jurisdiction**:\n-
**Federal level (FERC)**: Interstate transmission and wholesale markets.\n- **State level (PSCs)**:
Retail rates, local service, and infrastructure.\n- **Local level**: Rights-of-way, metering, and
service connections.\n\nUtilities must comply with **codes and standards** adopted by these
regulatory bodies. The **authority having jurisdiction (AHJ)** may grant exceptions for non-utility
installations, such as customer-owned conductors or equipment not under utility control.\n\nFor
specific projects, always consult the **local Public Service Commission**, **FERC**, or **FCC**,
depending on the nature of the installation and jurisdictional scope.' additional_kwargs={}
