  Message 8: content='North American contractors handle detailed design due to a combination of
industry practices, regulatory frameworks, and project delivery models. Key reasons include:\n\n1.
**Design-Build Delivery Model**: In North America, the design-build approach is widely used, where a
single contractor is responsible for both design and construction. This model streamlines project
delivery, reduces coordination delays, and enhances accountability.\n\n2. **Constructability
Expertise**: Contractors possess on-the-ground knowledge of construction methods, sequencing, and
site constraints. Their involvement in detailed design ensures that plans are practical, buildable,
and cost-effective.\n\n3. **Code Compliance and Risk Management**: Contractors are responsible for
ensuring that designs meet local, state, and federal codes (e.g., NEC, ASHRAE, IEEE standards).
Their involvement reduces the risk of non-compliance, rework, and project delays.\n\n4.
**Subcontractor Coordination**: Detailed design allows contractors to effectively coordinate with
specialized subcontractors (e.g., electrical, mechanical, structural), ensuring seamless integration
of systems and components.\n\n5. **Client Preferences and Project Efficiency**: Clients often prefer
a single point of responsibility, which the contractor provides. This reduces interface issues and
improves project timelines and budget control.\n\n6. **IEEE and Industry Standards**: Standards such
as IEEE 1100 (for power quality) and BICSI CO-OSP (for telecommunications infrastructure) emphasize
the importance of coordinated design and performance, which contractors are well-positioned to
manage.\n\nThese factors collectively make North American contractors central to the detailed design
phase, ensuring that projects are technically sound, compliant, and constructible.'
